From f5de88e6e32bfa065654e1eceb4c48da5cf6c808 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.local>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 12:50:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] Auth: Supabase sign-up, sign-in, profiles and family
 invitations
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

First version of accounts. Memorial pages stay completely public.

- src/lib/supabase.ts     client built from VITE_ env vars only. Null when
                          unconfigured, never throws, so a missing .env can
                          never take down the public memorial pages.
- src/lib/authContext.ts  context + types (own module, keeps fast refresh)
- src/lib/AuthProvider.tsx session, profile, signUp/signIn/signOut/update
- src/lib/useAuth.ts      hook
- src/pages/SignIn.tsx    email + password, register and sign in, ?mode=register
- src/pages/Account.tsx   profile (name, email) and family invitations
- Navbar                  Sign in / My account, hidden when unconfigured
- supabase/migrations/0001_auth_and_profiles.sql

SECURITY, deliberately:
- No key is hard-coded. .env and .env.local are now gitignored; .env.example
  documents what is needed.
- Vite inlines VITE_* into the browser bundle, so the anon key is NOT secret
  no matter where it is stored. What protects the data is Row Level Security,
  which the migration enables on both tables with owner-only policies. Without
  those policies the anon key would grant the world read and write access.
- The service_role key appears nowhere and must never be added.

INVITATIONS — a real limitation, not an oversight: sending an invitation email
via Supabase requires auth.admin.inviteUserByEmail(), which needs the
service_role key and therefore cannot run in a browser. This version records
the invitation in an owner-scoped table and gives the inviter a link to send
themselves. Having MemoryGlen send the mail needs a Supabase Edge Function
holding the service key server-side.

TESTS: six new, covering that Virginia, John Peters and the directory all
render for a signed-out visitor with no credentials present, that tabs still
switch on one click, that the sign-in page degrades gracefully, and that no
Supabase key is hard-coded anywhere in src/. Ten tests pass in total.

Includes the tab-content fix (blank panel until a second activation).
